<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Implementation Details:</span>
We invoke different LLMs as the base models in ComplexVCoder, including commercial LLMs (i.e., Deepseek-V3, GPT-3.5, GPT-4o, GPT-4o-mini), and open-source LLMs with smaller scale (i.e., Qwen2.5-Coder, Qwen2.5-32B, and Qwen2.5-14B).
We use Icarus Verilog <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">26</a>]</cite> and Pyverilog <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">24</a>]</cite> to check the syntactic correctness of the generated Verilog solutions.
If the design is syntactically correct, we proceed by running the corresponding test bench from the benchmark suite and comparing the output of the generated solution against the golden reference to verify its functional correctness.
During the instruction tuning process, we apply LoRA <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">29</a>]</cite> to enhance efficiency while preserving model performance. The learning rate <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>γ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma</annotation></semantics></math> is set to <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\times 10^{-4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\times 10^{-4}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the models are trained for 3 epochs. Both training and inference are conducted using eight NVIDIA 4090 GPUs.</p>
</div>
